# Operations improvement plan

## Executive recommendation

Do not attempt another across-the-board schedule compression. The previous failure likely removed elapsed time without removing the causes of delay, increasing overlap, expediting, and escapes.

Instead, launch a cross-functional lead-time program built on four principles:

1. **Measure the full order-to-delivery critical path using transaction timestamps**, separating processing time from queue, hold, rework, and approval time;
2. **Reduce queues, variability, batching, and material shortages**, rather than shortening or bypassing certified inspections;
3. **Segment the product and fulfillment model** so standard configurations can be supplied from prebuilt, certified modules while genuinely engineered orders retain longer lead times;
4. **Make production, quality, and sales jointly accountable** for fast, reliable delivery and field quality.

A credible phased ambition is:

- Reduce the current 14-week quote to **8–10 weeks for most repeat configurations within six months**;
- Reach **6–8 weeks for selected standard configurations within 9–12 months**;
- Maintain separate, evidence-based lead times for highly customized orders;
- Achieve at least **90–95% on-time delivery to the confirmed date** before shortening published quotes further.

The six-week competitor quote should be treated as a competitive benchmark, not as a commitment the company must immediately copy. The company should quote six weeks only where demonstrated capability supports it.

---

## 1. Determine where the 14 weeks are actually lost

### Build an order-level value-stream timeline

For the last 6–12 months, reconstruct the timeline for a representative sample of orders, including:

- Standard, configured, and engineered-to-order products;
- On-time and late orders;
- Orders with and without rework;
- High-volume and low-volume product families;
- Orders that received sales expedites;
- Orders associated with warranty claims.

Extract timestamps from the ERP, manufacturing execution, quality, procurement, engineering, and shipping systems. Where reliable timestamps do not exist, conduct floor observations and short-term manual tracking.

For every operation and quality gate, capture:

- Order entry and technical-release time;
- Engineering review and drawing/BOM release;
- Material requisition, purchase, receipt, and incoming inspection;
- Queue entry and operation start/finish;
- Setup and changeover;
- Batch waiting;
- Inspection request, inspector arrival, inspection completion, and record approval;
- Nonconformance discovery, disposition, rework, and reinspection;
- Final test, certification-document completion, packing, and shipment;
- Schedule changes, expedite decisions, and reasons for holds.

Create one timeline for **elapsed time** and another for **actual touch time**. An industrial product may require only days of fabrication, assembly, test, and inspection but spend weeks waiting.

### Classify every day of lead time

Assign elapsed time to a limited set of causes:

| Time category | Typical root cause |
|---|---|
| Processing | Actual fabrication, assembly, testing, inspection, or shipping work |
| Queue | Excess WIP, overloaded bottleneck, poor priority rules |
| Batch waiting | Work held until enough units accumulate for processing or inspection |
| Material waiting | Late supplier, inaccurate inventory, long purchase approval |
| Information waiting | Missing specification, drawing, BOM, customer approval |
| Quality hold | Inspector capacity, document delay, nonconformance disposition |
| Rework | Process instability, unclear work instructions, supplier defects |
| Schedule disruption | Expedites, forecast changes, sales promises, machine downtime |
| Finished-goods waiting | Shipping coordination, customer acceptance, paperwork |

Show the result as a lead-time waterfall. The first target should be the largest avoidable blocks of elapsed time, not necessarily the operations with the longest cycle time.

### Analyze flow and variability

For each major product family, measure:

- Median and 80th/90th-percentile lead time, not only the average;
- Queue time at each work center and quality gate;
- First-pass yield and rolled throughput yield;
- Rework hours and nonconformance aging;
- Supplier on-time-in-full performance and incoming defect rates;
- Engineering-release turnaround and BOM-error frequency;
- Schedule adherence and frequency of priority changes;
- Setup time, uptime, and demonstrated capacity at the constraint;
- WIP by process stage and age;
- Inspection workload and inspector response time;
- Warranty claims by failure mode and production period.

Use Little’s Law—lead time is approximately WIP divided by throughput—to identify areas where excess WIP is creating long queues. Adding more work to an already overloaded process may improve apparent utilization while worsening delivery time.

### Likely hypotheses to test

The diagnosis should not presume guilt, but common sources of lost time include:

- Orders entering production before specifications or BOMs are complete;
- Large transfer batches created to maximize local machine utilization;
- Standard and custom products competing under the same schedule;
- Long queues before bottleneck operations or certified inspection points;
- Inspectors called only after an entire batch is complete;
- Repeated rescheduling caused by sales expedites;
- Long-lead components ordered only after receipt of a customer order;
- Supplier defects discovered late in assembly;
- Rework held for engineering or material-review-board disposition;
- Certification records completed serially after physical testing;
- Too many product variants and uncontrolled engineering changes;
- Capacity planning based on theoretical rather than demonstrated output.

The diagnostic should be completed in approximately four weeks. Obvious low-risk fixes can begin immediately, but no quality gate should be changed until its regulatory and technical basis is understood.

---

## 2. Redesign the fulfillment model by product segment

A single lead-time promise is unlikely to be appropriate for every order.

### Segment products into fulfillment classes

| Class | Characteristics | Fulfillment model | Illustrative target |
|---|---|---|---:|
| Standard | Stable demand, standard options, repeatable routing | Assemble or finish to order from certified modules | 4–6 weeks |
| Configured | Approved combinations of standard modules | Configure to order with controlled option rules | 6–8 weeks |
| Engineered | New design, unusual requirements, customer approvals | Engineer to order with milestone-based commitment | 10–14+ weeks |

Actual targets should be set after the diagnostic and capacity validation.

Sales should present customers with explicit choices:

- Standard configuration at the shortest lead time;
- Approved options at a moderate lead time;
- Custom engineering at a longer lead time and appropriate price.

This can recover speed-sensitive deals without forcing every custom order through an unsafe six-week process.

### Use modularization and postponement

Where technically feasible:

- Standardize common frames, subassemblies, controls, harnesses, and interfaces;
- Prebuild stable, high-use modules;
- Complete module-level inspections and preserve certification records;
- Delay customer-specific configuration until late in the process;
- Use approved configuration rules to prevent incompatible combinations;
- Reduce unique components and consolidate low-value variants.

Prebuilding is appropriate only when modules have stable designs, manageable obsolescence risk, adequate traceability, and certification that remains valid through final integration. Final product inspection must still cover interfaces and system performance.

### Set inventory deliberately

Do not solve the problem through indiscriminate finished-goods inventory. Use a segmented policy:

- Hold safety stock for long-lead, common, stable components;
- Hold certified standard modules where demand is repeatable;
- Use supplier-owned or consigned inventory for appropriate items;
- Avoid stocking highly customized or frequently changing parts;
- Set reorder points using consumption, replenishment variability, and service targets;
- Review obsolete and excess inventory monthly.

Inventory should be placed at the point that decouples volatile customer demand from long upstream replenishment while preserving late-stage customization.

---

## 3. Improve planning, scheduling, and shop-floor flow

### Establish a controlled order-release gate

An order should not enter production until it is “ready,” including:

- Customer specifications are complete;
- Approved configuration or released engineering package exists;
- BOM and routing are validated;
- Required certifications and inspections are identified;
- Critical materials are available or have confirmed dates;
- Capacity is reserved at the constraint;
- The promised date has been checked against actual capacity.

Incomplete orders should remain in a visible preproduction queue rather than create hidden disruption on the floor.

### Replace infinite scheduling with finite-capacity commitments

Implement capable-to-promise scheduling based on:

- Demonstrated capacity rather than rated capacity;
- Actual setup and run times;
- Planned maintenance and realistic uptime;
- Inspector and test-bay capacity;
- Material availability;
- Existing committed orders;
- Bottleneck loading.

Sales may quote from approved lead-time bands, but exceptions should require a cross-functional capable-to-promise check. No individual function should be able to insert an expedite unilaterally.

### Stabilize the production schedule

Create:

- A short frozen production window, such as one to two weeks;
- A controlled planning window beyond it;
- Explicit rules for genuine customer emergencies;
- A visible cost and displacement impact for every expedite;
- One agreed priority list for production, quality, and engineering.

Frequent priority changes cause setups, partial work, missing materials, inspection conflicts, and errors. Expedites should be rare and approved by an accountable cross-functional owner.

### Control WIP and improve flow

For each product family:

- Set WIP caps at major process stages;
- Release new work only as downstream capacity becomes available;
- Use smaller transfer batches where technically and economically feasible;
- Create FIFO lanes and aging alerts;
- Establish standard work and point-of-use materials;
- Separate rework from normal flow while giving it a defined disposition time;
- Balance work around the true constraint;
- Cross-train operators at high-variability or capacity-sensitive steps.

The goal is not maximum utilization at every machine. Nonconstraint resources may need idle capacity to keep the overall system flowing.

### Improve the bottleneck

Once the true constraint is identified:

- Protect it from material and information shortages;
- Move preparation, paperwork, and setup activities offline;
- Sequence work to reduce changeovers without creating excessive batches;
- Add shifts or cross-trained resources before buying equipment;
- Reduce downtime through preventive maintenance;
- Reserve capacity for predictable inspection and rework;
- Add equipment only if the constraint remains after flow improvements.

Any capital request should show how much end-to-end lead time and throughput it changes, not merely local machine utilization.

### Accelerate engineering and nonconformance decisions

For configured and engineered orders:

- Use standard designs, approved option libraries, and reusable drawings;
- Establish service-level targets for technical review and drawing release;
- Apply revision control and prevent obsolete instructions at the workstation;
- Assign daily material-review-board time;
- Set escalation limits for aging nonconformances;
- Analyze recurring deviations and eliminate their causes rather than repeatedly granting concessions.

---

## 4. Protect and strengthen certified quality gates

Lead-time reduction must come primarily from removing waiting and defects, not from skipping inspection.

### Treat quality requirements as fixed constraints

Create a quality-gate register showing for each gate:

- Regulatory, certification, customer, or internal requirement;
- Required inspection or test;
- Qualified personnel and equipment;
- Required records and traceability;
- Prerequisites;
- Acceptance criteria;
- Reinspection requirements after rework;
- Whether sampling is permitted;
- Who can authorize a process change.

No gate may be removed, combined, or changed merely to meet a delivery target. Any change must undergo formal quality and regulatory review, validation where required, and document-control approval.

### Reduce waiting around inspections

Certified inspections can remain intact while elapsed time falls through:

- Booking inspections from the production schedule rather than calling after completion;
- Leveling inspection demand;
- Cross-training and certifying additional inspectors where standards permit;
- Providing inspection fixtures, calibrated tools, and records in advance;
- Digitizing travelers and certification documents;
- Capturing results at the point of inspection;
- Completing documentation in parallel with downstream preparation;
- Using dedicated inspection capacity at high-volume cells;
- Prequalifying external inspection capacity for peaks, if permissible.

Measure both inspection processing time and **request-to-completion time**. A 30-minute inspection with a three-day queue is primarily a scheduling problem.

### Build quality into the process

Strengthen upstream controls through:

- Updated process failure mode and effects analysis;
- Control plans linked to the routing;
- Clear standard work and visual acceptance criteria;
- Error-proofing and automated parameter checks;
- Tool calibration and measurement-system analysis;
- First-piece verification after setup or changeover;
- Statistical process control on critical characteristics;
- Supplier process controls for critical parts;
- Operator self-checks that supplement, but do not replace, required independent inspection;
- Full component, operator, process, and test traceability.

The objective is to arrive at each certified gate with a conforming product, not to rely on the gate to sort defects.

### Use risk-based inspection only with formal authorization

Inspection frequency may be optimized only where standards, customer contracts, and regulatory requirements allow it. Any move from full inspection to sampling should require:

- Stable capability data;
- Sufficient historical performance;
- Approved sampling plans;
- Documented risk assessment;
- Customer or certification-body consent where required;
- Defined triggers for return to increased inspection.

### Prevent another warranty failure

Before a faster process becomes standard:

- Complete process-validation runs;
- Verify first-pass yield and critical capability;
- Conduct an enhanced final audit on pilot units;
- Track serial numbers and process histories;
- Monitor early field performance;
- Hold daily review of escapes and high-severity defects;
- Stop expansion if quality thresholds deteriorate.

Quality should retain independent stop-ship authority. Using it should trigger rapid executive resolution, not retaliation for missed delivery.

---

## 5. Improve supplier performance and material availability

### Segment suppliers and parts

Classify parts by:

- Lead time;
- Criticality to quality and certification;
- Spend;
- Supply risk;
- Demand stability;
- Availability of substitutes.

Focus first on components that frequently stop the critical path.

### Actions for critical suppliers

- Share a stable forecast and defined frozen horizon;
- Establish blanket purchase orders with scheduled releases;
- Negotiate reserved capacity for long-lead parts;
- Use consignment or vendor-managed inventory where appropriate;
- Qualify second sources for high-risk components;
- Standardize components across product variants;
- Agree on quality plans, traceability, and change-notification requirements;
- Track on-time-in-full delivery and defect rates;
- Conduct supplier process audits and corrective actions;
- Move incoming verification upstream to the supplier only after demonstrated capability and formal approval.

Do not reduce incoming inspection for critical parts simply because it saves time. Supplier certification should be based on process evidence and sustained performance.

### Improve internal material control

- Validate BOM accuracy;
- Use barcode or serial tracking;
- Kit material before order release;
- Flag shortages before the scheduled start;
- Separate suspected or nonconforming material physically and electronically;
- Count high-risk inventory more frequently;
- Identify substitute parts only through approved engineering and quality processes.

---

## 6. Align production, quality, and sales

The feud is partly a governance and incentive problem. If sales is rewarded for bookings, production for utilization, and quality for finding or preventing any possible risk regardless of response time, each function can optimize its metric while harming the customer outcome.

### Create one shared objective

Establish a cross-functional program led by a senior executive who owns the full order-to-delivery process. The core team should include production, quality, sales, engineering, supply chain, service/warranty, and finance.

Adopt a shared scorecard:

| Outcome | Metric |
|---|---|
| Speed | Median and 90th-percentile order-to-ship lead time |
| Reliability | On-time-in-full delivery to confirmed date |
| Quality | First-pass yield, escape rate, warranty claims, cost of poor quality |
| Flow | WIP, queue age, schedule adherence |
| Commercial | Deals lost on lead time, standard-option adoption, expedite rate |
| Supply | Critical-part availability and supplier on-time-in-full performance |

No lead-time target is achieved if warranty or escape thresholds deteriorate.

### Use paired metrics to prevent gaming

Examples include:

- Lead time **and** first-pass yield;
- On-time delivery **and** warranty rate;
- Throughput **and** schedule adherence;
- Sales bookings **and** quote accuracy;
- Inspector response time **and** escape rate;
- Supplier delivery **and** incoming quality.

This prevents production from pushing defective units forward, quality from allowing queues to accumulate, and sales from promising dates the factory cannot achieve.

### Clarify decision rights

- **Sales:** selects from approved lead-time bands and supplies complete customer requirements;
- **Planning:** owns capable-to-promise dates and the master schedule;
- **Production:** owns adherence, flow, standard work, and first-time process execution;
- **Quality:** owns gate criteria, release authority, certification compliance, and CAPA;
- **Engineering:** owns timely release and controlled design changes;
- **Executive sponsor:** resolves tradeoffs and approves exceptions.

Neither sales nor production can waive a quality gate. Quality cannot add undocumented inspection requirements without a risk basis and change control.

### Introduce a no-blame operating cadence

Use:

- A daily 15-minute flow meeting for shortages, holds, and constraint risks;
- A weekly order-review meeting covering capacity and customer commitments;
- A weekly quality/flow review of defects, rework, and aging holds;
- A monthly steering committee for performance, investments, and policy decisions.

Problems should be coded by root cause and process owner, not by which department discovered them. Warranty and delivery failures should use structured root-cause analysis with jointly owned corrective actions.

### Align incentives

A meaningful portion of variable compensation for all three leaders should depend on the same balanced outcomes:

- On-time-in-full delivery;
- Lead-time reduction;
- First-pass yield;
- Warranty/escape performance;
- Quote-date accuracy.

Local measures such as machine utilization, bookings, or inspection volume should be secondary.

---

## 7. Phased implementation

### First 30 days: Stabilize and diagnose

- Appoint the executive sponsor and value-stream owner;
- Stop informal expedite practices;
- Preserve all certified gates;
- Map order histories and quantify elapsed-time categories;
- Identify the bottleneck and top five queue causes;
- Separate standard, configured, and engineered orders;
- Establish baseline delivery, yield, warranty, and WIP metrics;
- Select one high-volume, technically stable product family for a pilot;
- Review unfinished orders for missing engineering, material, or quality prerequisites.

Immediate low-risk actions may include prebooking inspectors, daily nonconformance disposition, shortage escalation, and improved order-entry completeness.

### Days 31–90: Pilot the redesigned flow

For the pilot family:

- Implement a ready-to-release order gate;
- Establish finite-capacity scheduling and a frozen window;
- Set WIP caps and FIFO lanes;
- Reduce transfer batches;
- Kit materials before release;
- Schedule inspections as part of the routing;
- Add certified inspector capacity if data shows a genuine constraint;
- Update standard work, PFMEA, and control plans;
- Set supplier agreements for critical components;
- Begin prebuilding approved common modules;
- Offer sales a controlled fast-lead-time configuration.

Run several consecutive production cycles and compare them with the baseline.

### Months 4–6: Scale proven changes

Scale only if the pilot shows:

- Material lead-time reduction;
- Stable or improved first-pass yield;
- No increase in escapes or high-severity nonconformances;
- Predictable schedule performance;
- Complete certification records;
- No adverse early field signal.

Expand to adjacent product families, rationalize options, qualify suppliers, and adjust capacity at proven constraints. At this stage, the company may reduce selected quoted lead times to 8–10 weeks.

### Months 7–12: Institutionalize and approach competitive lead times

- Expand modular design and postponement;
- Improve configuration tools;
- Complete targeted automation or constraint investments;
- Establish supplier certification where justified;
- Improve digital traceability and scheduling integration;
- Validate 6–8-week performance for eligible configurations;
- Update commercial pricing and lead-time policies.

Published lead times should be based on sustained 80th- or 90th-percentile performance, not the best observed order.

---

## 8. Pilot acceptance and stop conditions

The pilot should have explicit release criteria. Illustratively:

- At least 90–95% on-time delivery to the confirmed date;
- Target lead time achieved across multiple consecutive cycles;
- First-pass yield no worse than baseline and trending upward;
- No missed or incomplete certified inspection;
- No increase in defect escapes;
- Critical process capability remains within approved limits;
- Rework and nonconformance aging decline;
- Certification and traceability records are complete;
- Early warranty signals remain within control limits.

Pause or reverse the rollout if:

- Any certified gate is bypassed;
- Critical-characteristic capability declines;
- High-severity defects or escapes increase;
- Rework rises materially;
- Workers report pressure to conceal or defer defects;
- Lead-time improvement depends on repeated overtime or expediting;
- The fast lane causes deterioration across the rest of the factory;
- Supplier substitutions occur without approval;
- Capacity or delivery performance cannot support the revised quote reliably.

## Bottom line

The company should not choose between speed and quality. It should remove the weeks spent waiting for materials, decisions, capacity, inspections, and schedule stability while retaining every required quality control.

The most important changes are to:

- Expose the actual order-level critical path;
- Segment standard from custom demand;
- Prebuild certified common modules;
- Release only complete, material-ready orders;
- Schedule against finite capacity;
- Limit WIP and priority changes;
- Integrate inspection capacity into the production plan;
- Eliminate recurring defects upstream;
- Secure critical supplier capacity;
- Give production, quality, and sales one balanced scorecard and one commitment process.

This approach can create a defensible short-lead-time offer for standard products without repeating the prior warranty failure or making an unsafe six-week promise across the entire portfolio.

